    "New_PyDay01Audio03.mp3": {
      "src": "Day01/New_PyDay01Audio03.mp3",
      "duration": 56.26,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "In Python, every value is an object living in your computer's memory, and every object has an identity: its memory address.",
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 8.04
        },
        {
          "text": "The big question is whether that object can be changed after it is created.",
          "start": 8.58,
          "end": 12.42
        },
        {
          "text": "The answer splits Python's data types into two families: Mutable objects and Immutable objects.",
          "start": 12.88,
          "end": 18.7
        },
        {
          "text": "Mutable objects, such as lists, dictionaries, and sets, can be modified in-place without changing their memory address.",
          "start": 19.22,
          "end": 26.74
        },
        {
          "text": "Immutable objects, including integers, floats, strings, and tuples, can never be changed once created.",
          "start": 26.74,
          "end": 34.3
        },
        {
          "text": "Any modification generates a brand new object at a new memory address.",
          "start": 34.84,
          "end": 38.9
        },
        {
          "text": "Watch out for the dangerous Pitfall: writing clean data equals raw data does not create a copy, it creates an alias pointing to the exact same memory!",
          "start": 39.42,
          "end": 48.42
        },
        {
          "text": "Modifying clean data will silently corrupt your raw source.",
          "start": 49.08,
          "end": 52.08
        },
        {
          "text": "Always use dot copy to safeguard your analytical pipelines.",
          "start": 52.56,
          "end": 55.72
        }
      ],
      "cues": [
        {
          "atSec": 0.0,
          "selector": "#headingMutability",
