    "New_PyDay01Audio05.mp3": {
      "src": "Day01/New_PyDay01Audio05.mp3",
      "duration": 50.06,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Let's trace how Python manages memory under the hood through our runtime execution pipeline.",
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 5.26
        },
        {
          "text": "When you run Python code, Python first allocates object values inside heap memory.",
          "start": 5.76,
          "end": 10.78
        },
        {
          "text": "Variables are not boxes that hold values; they are lightweight pointers or name tags bound to memory addresses.",
          "start": 11.34,
          "end": 17.92
        },
        {
          "text": "When two variables are assigned the same value, they point to the exact same object in RAM.",
          "start": 18.5,
          "end": 23.78
        },
        {
          "text": "Reassigning a variable does not overwrite the old data; it simply repoints the tag to a new memory location.",
          "start": 24.38,
          "end": 30.7
        },
        {
          "text": "To test whether two variables share the exact same memory address, use the is keyword, which checks object identity via memory IDs, compared to double equals, which checks value equality.",
          "start": 31.16,
          "end": 43.2
        },
        {
          "text": "Python automatically frees unused memory using reference counting and a cyclic garbage collector.",
          "start": 44.06,
          "end": 49.6
        }
      ],
      "cues": [
